1. Prerequisites
You will need to install Node.js on your computer first. You can get it at nodejs.org.

2. The Setup
Create a new folder for your project and create these three files inside it:

File 1: package.json
This tells Node.js what your app is and what it needs to run.

-- CODE STARTS HERE
{
  "name": "cheat-browser",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "main": "main.js",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "electron ."
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "electron": "^28.0.0"
  }
}

-- ENDS HERE 

File 2: main.js (The Brain)
This is the file that creates the window. Notice the webSecurity: false line—that is the magic switch that makes Blooket cheats and scripts work.

-- CODE STARTS HERE
const { app, BrowserWindow } = require('electron');
const path = require('path');

function createWindow() {
  const win = new BrowserWindow({
    width: 1200,
    height: 800,
    webPreferences: {
      nodeIntegration: true,
      contextIsolation: false,
      // THIS IS THE KEY: It disables the security that blocks your scripts
      webSecurity: false 
    }
  });

  // This loads your existing HTML browser file
  win.loadFile('index.html');
}

app.whenReady().then(createWindow);

app.on('window-all-closed', () => {
  if (process.platform !== 'darwin') app.quit();
});
-- ends here 

File 3: index.html (Your Browser UI)
You can use the full script I gave you earlier, but with one huge change: you can now remove the "Bypass Mode" logic and change the applyCustomCSS function to be much more powerful.

In your <script> tag inside index.html, update the function to this:

-- STARTS HERE 

function applyCustomCSS() {
    const cssCode = document.getElementById('css-input').value;
    const activeFrame = document.getElementById('frame-' + activeTabId);
    
    // In Electron with webSecurity: false, this will NEVER fail.
    const style = activeFrame.contentDocument.createElement('style');
    style.innerHTML = cssCode;
    activeFrame.contentDocument.head.appendChild(style);
}

-- ENDS HERE

. How to Run It
Open your terminal or command prompt.

Navigate to your folder (cd path/to/folder).

Type npm install (this downloads the Electron engine).

Type npm start.
